   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the tax law, in relation to increasing and providing for the\ndistribution of the revenue from extending the authorization for the hotel and\nmotel taxes in Suffolk County",
    "basePrintNo": "S9318",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9318-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1202-o, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9318\n\nSPONSOR: BROOKS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the tax law, in relation to increasing and providing for\nthe distribution of the revenue from extending the authorization for the\nhotel and motel taxes in Suffolk County\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this legislation is to increase the hotel and motel taxes\nof Suffolk County, expand the permissible uses of the revenues generated\nby such taxes, and removes the expiration date for the imposition of\nsuch taxes in the County.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill amends subdivisions 1, 6 and 7 of section 1202-o of the tax\nlaw to increase the hotel and motel tax in Suffolk County from 3% to 5\n1/2%, sets forth a new formula for the distribution of the revenues\ngenerated from the tax, including but not limited to funds for a new\nSuffolk County Infrastructure Fund, and removes the expiration date for\nthe imposition of the tax in Suffolk County.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 1992, Suffolk County requested the ability to impose a lodging tax to\nprovide a dedicated and sustained funding source for the promotion of\ntourism to the County. Since that time, the proceeds from the lodging\ntax have been used to promote tourism in Suffolk County and helped fund\nmany of the County's historic sites, museums, parks, and cultural\nprogramming. Currently, Suffolk County's lodging tax is one of the\nlowest in New York State, and many other counties in New York State have\nthe authority to impose a lodging tax of 5%. The additional revenue\ngenerated from an increase in Suffolk County's lodging tax as well as\nthe expansion of the permissible uses of the revenues generated by the\ntax would preserve and further enhance the County's ability to remain an\nexcelldfit tourism destination and attract new travel and tourism busi-\nness. Additionally, the funding of a new Suffolk County Infrastructure\nFund will result in the creation of Suffolk County's first, much needed\nconvention center. Long Island and its natural assets have made the\nregion a thriving destination for leisure travelers who generate over\n$760 million in local and state taxes annually. The development of a\nconvention center located next to the region's only commercial airport\nand the county's highest utilized rail station will create thousands of\njobs and ultimately draw additional tourism business to the Long Island\nRegion and help to overcome the region's existing competitive disadvan-\ntage to business travel. According to US Travel, business travelers\naccount for 40%-60% of lodging and air travel and the convention center\nat this site will help to generate such economic activity and also\nfacilitate airport improvements.  Furthermore, the removal of the expi-\nration date from the imposition of the County's lodging tax will align\nthe County with the overwhelming majority of other counties in New York\nState whose ability to impose a lodging tax is not limited by a specific\ntime period.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately\n",
